<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Lewis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1940">1940</a>–<a href="/years/2020">2020</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Lewis was an American civil rights activist and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> House of Representatives for an Atlanta district from 1987 until his death. He joined the Nashville sit-ins in 1960 and was beaten as one of the original Freedom Riders. He chaired the Student Nonviolent Coordinating Committee from 1963 to 1966 and was one of the &quot;Big Six&quot; leaders of the March on Washington. On &quot;Bloody Sunday&quot; in 1965 he led marchers across Selma's Edmund Pettus Bridge, where state troopers and police attacked them and fractured his skull.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-lewis.jpg" alt="John Lewis" width="320" height="462" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States House of Representatives · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Lewis-2006_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
